2. Sinne is the second burthen of our lives, and much the greatest;
§. 6 Unto this, is this Consolation peculiarly suited: So Heb. 6. 17, 18. an Allusion is taken from the man stayer under the Low, who having killed a man at unawares, and brought the guilt of his blood upon him selfe, fled with speed for his deliverance to the Citty of refuge: our great and only refuge from the guilt of sinne, is the Lord Jesus Christ in our flying to him, doth the spirit administer consolation to us. A sense of sin fills the heart with troubles and disquietnesse; it is the Holy Ghost, which gives us peace in Christ. That gives an apprehension of wrath, the Holy Ghost sheds abroad the Love of God in our hearts. From thence doth Satan, and the Low accuse us, as objects of Gods hatred, the spirit beares witnesse with our spirits, that we are the Children of God. There is not any one engine
or instrument, that sin useth, or sets up against our peace, but one effect or other of the Holy Ghost towards us, is suited and fitted to the casting of it downe.
